  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nWe address the minimization of $\\|f * f\\|_\\infty / \\sum(f)^2$ by directly optimizing the max-value of the convolution. Unlike previous approaches, we avoid surrogate problems and instead use iterative optimization with multi-start initialization, adaptive resampling, and goal-oriented parameter tuning. This method is material different because it focuses solely on the peak of the convolution through bounded optimization, never approximating it via logarithmic or spectral means.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\nWe operate in the positive orthant ($f_i \\geq 0$) of coefficients $\\mathbb{R}_{+}^n$ with normalization $\\sum f = \\sqrt{2n}$ (as in the reference). The max-convolution value $M = \\|f*f\\|_\\infty$ is not abstracted but directly minimized using gradient-based optimization (Adam) with carefully constructed linear constraints derived from the autocorrelation property.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\nFor each candidate length $n$: [256, 512, 768, 1024, 2048], we initialize several distinct sequences (unit-normalized versions of hard windows, super-Gaussians, and uniform interpolation). For each initialization $f_0$:\n   - Assess internal priority based on early scaling characteristics\n   - Perform gradient descent projection with adaptive normalization\n   - Convert to projective space projection to maintain consistent peak-minimum\n   - Monitor changes in both $MaxConv$ and $TuningWeight$\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\nWe consider a small set of meaningful variance points - 5 per candidate length, roughly equating to 3-6 total evaluations per $n$. Sequences are dimension-reduced systematically via spectral projection where possible. The optimization formula is:\n   $f \\leftarrow f \\cdot \\exp(-\\beta \\cdot (convolution(\\Omega)))$\nwhere $\\Omega$ is the optimized triangular combination matrix.\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\nThe cost of each evaluation is roughly aneomorphic no-go as follows:\n   - Uniform-normalized base: O(n log n)\n   - Super-Gaussian initialization: O(n) for each step\n   - Adam tuning: O(n) per evaluate\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\nThe sequence returns when:\n   - $Time < 1500$ and continuous improvement is detected\n   - $Time >= 1500$ - after quantization of quality headers, the latest winner is returned\nHalf-valid candidates undergo evaluation skipping unless the initial calculation indicates improvement.",
